 "note": "Maps every bed and cue name in the cue sheet (out/cuesheet.json from tools/cuesheet.js, or audio/cues_draft.json until it exists) to sound. Cues: rel_db is the sound's loudest 100 ms (K-weighted) relative to the narration's integrated loudness; class says whether it is a story effect (heard about 12-15 dB under the voice, so it carries on a phone speaker) or a pop (pops, whooshes, card lands and UI ticks: 20 dB or more under); build_mix.py reports each one as heard (its loudest 400 ms against the voice's 3 s loudness around it). align peak puts the sound's loudest point on the cue time, start its onset; pan: from the cue's x (pixels of a 1920 frame, or 0..1), at most \u00b10.3, else centre (a cue's p.pan wins); after_speech S: start where the narration's current word ends, if that is within S s (the sneeze, the cry and the stings land in the pauses); cycle rotates files by occurrence; steps lays a cue's p.n steps at its p.gap; per_n_s makes a cue p.n x per_n_s long (typing, the calculator); a cue's p.gain_db or p.level adds to rel_db. Beds: the music follows beds.music.timeline (phrases on the narration clock: which variation of audio/score_plan.json plays from where to where, null for a stop-down), wherever the cue sheet has a music bed; a bed with a file is an effect bed (the party murmur, rel_db = its integrated loudness re the narration); null is silent on purpose. aliases and bed_aliases map other names. Same-name cues closer than 150 ms play once.",
